Six Style Rules (Before / After)
 
1. Rule 1: Use active voice for instructions
Before: A new repository is created by you.
After: Create a new repository.

2. Rule 2: Keep sentences short (one action per sentence)
Before: You can click the plus icon at the top right and then choose new repository to start the task.
After: Click the + icon in the top‑right corner. Select New repository.

3. Rule 3: Use imperative verbs for step openings
Before: The next thing you do is type your repository name.
After: Enter your repository name.

4. Rule 4: Avoid vague words; name UI elements exactly
Before: Pick the secret option for the repo.
After: Select the Private option.

5. Rule 5: Remove redundant explanatory words
Before: In order to add a README file, you need to tick the small box.
After: Check Add a README file.

6. Rule 6: Use consistent technical terms
Before: folder / code space
After: repository
 
Revised Guide
 
Prerequisites
 
1. A signed‑in GitHub account.

2. A desktop web browser.

3. A valid repository name.
 
Steps
 
1. On the GitHub homepage, click the + icon in the top‑right corner. Select New repository.

2. Enter your repository name in the name field.

3. Choose Private to set repository visibility.

4. Check Add a README file.

5. Click Create repository.
 
Result Check
 
Open your repository page. Confirm the README.md file appears on the main page.
 
Record of Six Changes
 
1. Changed passive sentence “A new repository is created by you” to active imperative “Create a new repository.”

2. Split one long mixed sentence into two short separate action sentences.

3. Rewrote “The next thing you do is type your repository name” to “Enter your repository name.”

4. Replaced vague “secret option” with exact UI label “Private option.”

5. Cut redundant phrase “In order to, you need to” and simplified the sentence.

6. Unified inconsistent “folder / code space” to standard term “repository.”
 
